WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.955
Time to build.

2
00:00:01.975 --> 00:00:04.067
This is the biggest station on the exam.

3
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.855
Nine tools and ideas.

4
00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:08.855
We go fast, one picture each.

5
00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.017
One: Pub/Sub.

6
00:00:10.037 --> 00:00:12.315
Think of a post office for events.

7
00:00:12.335 --> 00:00:14.827
Publishers drop messages on a topic.

8
00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:17.575
Every subscription gets its own copy.

9
00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:19.225
Delivery is at least once.

10
00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:20.638
So duplicates will happen.

11
00:00:20.658 --> 00:00:22.035
Plan for them.

12
00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:25.385
A subscriber must say thanks, or ack, within the deadline.

13
00:00:25.405 --> 00:00:27.545
The default is 10 seconds.

14
00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:28.220
Need order?

15
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.420
Use ordering keys.

16
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.433
Made a mistake?

17
00:00:30.453 --> 00:00:32.780
Seek back in time, and replay.

18
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.655
And to copy messages into BigQuery unchanged, use a BigQuery subscription.

19
00:00:37.675 --> 00:00:40.055
No Dataflow needed.

20
00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:43.390
Two: Apache Beam, the language of Dataflow.

21
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.278
A PCollection is the data.

22
00:00:45.298 --> 00:00:46.965
A PTransform is a step.

23
00:00:46.985 --> 00:00:49.375
And the pipeline is the whole graph.

24
00:00:49.395 --> 00:00:54.110
ParDo works on one element at a time, like a worker on an assembly line.

25
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.260
To add things up, use Combine.

26
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.195
It pre-adds on each worker, so it scales better than GroupByKey.

27
00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:05.320
A side input is a small lookup table that every worker can read.

28
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.607
Three: windows.

29
00:01:06.627 --> 00:01:09.655
A stream never ends, so you cut it into pieces.

30
00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:12.268
Always cut by event time: when it happened.

31
00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:13.940
Not when it arrived.

32
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.815
Fixed windows are like tiles.

33
00:01:15.835 --> 00:01:17.328
Every five minutes, a new one.

34
00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:18.850
No overlap.

35
00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.525
Sliding windows overlap.

36
00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:23.660
The last hour, updated every five minutes.

37
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.760
Session windows follow the user.

38
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.545
They close after a gap with no activity.

39
00:01:28.565 --> 00:01:32.582
The watermark is Dataflow's guess that all data up to a time has arrived.

40
00:01:32.602 --> 00:01:34.955
After that, data is late.

41
00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:38.218
The trap: a fixed window for no activity for an hour.

42
00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:40.705
That is a session window.

43
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:43.415
Four: running Dataflow jobs safely.

44
00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:46.715
To stop a streaming job without losing data, drain it.

45
00:01:46.735 --> 00:01:48.925
It finishes what is in flight.

46
00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:52.385
Cancel stops at once, and in-flight data is lost.

47
00:01:52.405 --> 00:01:55.820
To ship a new version and keep the state, use update.

48
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.245
For batch jobs that can wait, FlexRS is cheaper.

49
00:01:59.265 --> 00:02:02.720
And flex templates let others launch your job.

50
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.130
Five: Dataproc, for teams that already have Spark.

51
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.518
Best practice: one cluster per job.

52
00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:11.015
Create it, run the job, delete it.

53
00:02:11.035 --> 00:02:15.575
That works because the data lives in Cloud Storage, not on the cluster.

54
00:02:15.595 --> 00:02:18.050
Add cheap Spot workers for extra compute.

55
00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.110
But never keep data on them.

56
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.260
Or skip the cluster completely, with Dataproc Serverless.

57
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.870
Six: getting data into BigQuery.

58
00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.608
Batch load jobs are free.

59
00:02:28.628 --> 00:02:31.280
Use them when minutes of delay are fine.

60
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.080
For streaming, use the Storage Write API.

61
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.490
It gives exactly-once delivery.

62
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.165
For files, Avro and Parquet load fastest.

63
00:02:39.185 --> 00:02:41.175
They carry their own schema.

64
00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:44.588
The trap: zipping CSV files to speed things up.

65
00:02:44.608 --> 00:02:48.100
A gzip file cannot be read in parallel.

66
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.310
Seven, eight and nine, quickly.

67
00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.248
Kafka: keep it if your apps already speak Kafka, on the managed service.

68
00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:56.220
Otherwise, Pub/Sub.

69
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.870
Duplicates: remove them by key, with ROW_NUMBER or MERGE.

70
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.480
And never drop bad rows silently.

71
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.018
And AI: bring the model to the data.

72
00:03:05.038 --> 00:03:10.405
Call Gemini from SQL in BigQuery, or use RunInference in Dataflow.

73
00:03:10.425 --> 00:03:11.030
Recap.

74
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.940
The four you must not forget.

75
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.677
Pub/Sub means at least once.

76
00:03:14.697 --> 00:03:16.700
Windows use event time.

77
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.200
Drain to stop safely.

78
00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.960
And batch loads into BigQuery are free.

79
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.435
Nine checkpoints wait below.

80
00:03:22.455 --> 00:03:24.730
Take your time.
